{"success": true, "name": "reasoning/arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/marker-propagation-boundary-validation.md", "content": "# Marker propagation and boundary validation

In noisy ARC grids with one or more special-color seeds, do not assume the marker spreads through every cell that is not another color. A useful competing mechanism is flood-fill through only the designated background color, with other non-background colors acting as barriers. Simulate both candidate connectivity domains and compare the complete predicted footprint against all demo outputs, including untouched cells and grid-edge contact. Preserve original seeds and all cells outside the proven domain. Only project a propagation rule onto the test after reproducing each demo exactly; check shape, row widths, palette, and changed-cell count mechanically.

A propagation footprint that is surprisingly large is not itself evidence for unrestricted fill. Determine whether the allowed-color component is enclosed or connected to the boundary. If demonstrations fail to distinguish allowed-color connectivity from unrestricted flood fill, spend a paid demo only if those hypotheses generate materially different test outputs and a new example is likely to settle the distinction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/marker-propagation-boundary-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "reasoning/arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/structured-outline-validation.md", "content": "# Validate gap-completion rules in sparse line-and-outline grids

If demos recolor background cells between same-colored markers, avoid assuming that every horizontal and vertical alignment is independently interpolated. That rule can accidentally fill numerous cells where alignments cross, overlap, or belong to distinct fragments. Transcribe the changed-cell set in every demonstration, then test whether fills belong to a coherent outline/object or to selected segments; record aligned gaps left untouched as negative evidence. Replay the complete rule, including all unchanged cells, before applying it to a test.

A wrong global-interpolation submission falsifies that complete hypothesis. Reassess object structure and marker roles instead of repeating the same strategy with a changed color or a narrower guessed subset. Spend remaining demo budget only when a specific candidate distinction would change the predicted test grid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/structured-outline-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "reasoning/arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reassessment.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demo stream: evidence and reassessment

This session contained seven demonstrations with markedly different dimensions and structures (stripes, isolated markers, directional lines, and sparse motifs), followed by a 5×17 test. Repeated demo requests and reference reads did not establish a common transformation. The submitted test guess recolored the 9-background and 6 bands by an unsupported extrapolation and was incorrect. No solved transformation was established.

Reusable process lesson: classify each pair's geometry and roles before seeking a common rule. Track exact changed-cell regions, retained markers, background/boundary colors, and informative unchanged areas. Palette overlap alone is not a bridge between geometries. A viable candidate must replay every pair, including negative evidence, and fully specify how it projects to the test. If candidates remain, compare their complete test grids and spend a demo only for a concrete discriminator that could change the decision. After a wrong answer, reject the full rule and rebuild from all pairs; do not tweak cells on the submitted output. This note documents process, not a task solution.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "reasoning/arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/repeated-motif-extension.md", "content": "# Repeated motif, continuation, and extension: review notes

A single demonstration showed a structured foreground motif repeated in a second location; only a region at the later instance's edge changed to color 1. Other demonstrations showed the same broad palette but dissimilar geometry and localized changed regions. This does not establish a reliable shared operation by itself: the hypothesis that color 1 simply fills a horizontal run from the rightmost marks failed on the test, as did a second attempt that filled apparent gaps between sparse cells.

For future analogous tasks, compare exact repeated foreground masks before interpreting each changed run independently. Test whether copies differ by a precise template completion, periodic continuation, or directional extension, and verify the candidates against all demos and negative evidence. Do not use these session examples as a solved rule. After an incorrect grid, discard the complete inferred transformation and reconstruct deltas anew. Spend demos on an explicit discriminator and retain the exact one-action JSON contract.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/repeated-motif-extension.md"}

